// System memory: the buffer object every data movement in this model starts or
// ends with, and the plain data that describes it.
//
// One file, because these types are looked up by NAME from wherever they are
// used and are never included selectively (coding guidelines rule 1). There is
// no build that wants the fill pattern enum but not the block it describes.
package mem_pkg {
    import addr_reg_pkg::*;

    // What a memory block CONTAINS, described well enough that a consumer can
    // predict every word of it (mem_c/functions/pattern_word.pss).
    //
    // Carried in the buffer rather than passed between actions, because the
    // producer and the consumer of a block are usually in different components
    // -- and because a copy has to be able to say "the destination now holds
    // what the source held", which is a statement about the object, not about
    // either action.
    //
    // UNKNOWN is the one that earns its place. Data that arrived over the SPI
    // wire was produced by a device this model does not model, so it is not
    // predictable and shall not be checked. Making that a value of this enum,
    // and constraining check_a to reject it, turns "you cannot check received
    // data without a slave model" from a comment into an unschedulable
    // scenario.
    enum mem_pattern_e {
        MEM_PATTERN_ZERO,       // all words zero
        MEM_PATTERN_INCR,       // word i == seed + i
        MEM_PATTERN_PRAND,      // word i == a reproducible hash of seed and i
        MEM_PATTERN_UNKNOWN     // produced by the DUT; contents not predictable
    }

    // A block of system memory: THE unit of data this system moves.
    //
    // A `buffer` rather than a `stream` or a `struct`, deliberately:
    //
    //   * buffer   a consumer may not begin until the producer COMPLETES, which
    //              is exactly the semantics of "fill it, then DMA it, then check
    //              it". The ordering in every scenario below comes from this,
    //              not from the shape of an activity block.
    //   * not a stream, because a filled block persists and may have several
    //     consumers -- a stream permits exactly one.
    //   * not a struct, because it carries scheduling meaning.
    //
    // THE CLAIM IS THE ALLOCATION. Declaring a claim inside a flow object causes
    // allocation when the object is instantiated (§21.11), so each block gets
    // its own addresses and two blocks live simultaneously never overlap. That
    // is what makes a memory-to-memory copy a copy rather than a no-op, and it
    // is why the claim lives here rather than in each action that touches a
    // block: an action-local claim would give the producer and the consumer
    // different memory.
    //
    // The claim is TRANSPARENT so a scenario may constrain the address (see
    // pss_top: the system RAM region is added to a transparent space). Nothing
    // here does, but the alignment and size rules below need the size to be
    // solvable, and a transparent claim keeps the address available to a
    // scenario that wants to place a block deliberately.
    buffer mem_blk_s {
        rand transparent_addr_claim_s<>  mem;
        rand mem_pattern_e               pattern;
        rand bit[32]                     seed;

        // The transfer sizes this system supports. A DMA length register is
        // 32 bits, but nothing here needs to model a transfer bigger than a
        // few pages, and an unbounded size makes every scenario slow to solve.
        constraint size_c {
            mem.size in [4..4096];
            mem.size % 4 == 0;          // whole words: every engine here is 32-bit
        }

        // Word alignment is a DEVICE rule, not a preference: both the DMA
        // address registers and the SPI FIFO path move whole 32-bit words.
        constraint align_c {
            mem.alignment == 4;
        }
    }
}
